'''''Herpetogaster''''' is an [[extinct]] [[cambroernid]] [[genus]] of animal from the Early ([[Cambrian Stage 3|Stage 3]]) to Middle ([[Wuliuan]]) [[Cambrian]].<ref>{{harvnb|Yang|Kimmig|Schiffbauer|Peng|2023|p=2}}</ref>  As the earliest-diverging cambroernid, it is notable for being at the base of the known [[Ambulacraria]].<ref>{{harvnb|Yang|Kimmig|Schiffbauer|Peng|2023|p=10}}</ref> 

==Etymology==
The discoverers of ''Herpetogaster'' nicknamed it "creeposaurus", and the generic name (Greek, ''herpeto'') is a reference to this, as well as to its prominent stomach (Greek, ''gaster'').  The specific name of the type species ''H. collinsi'' honors the leader of the expiditions when the first specimens were collected, Desmond Collins.<ref>{{harvnb|Caron|Conway Morris|Shu|2010|p=2}}</ref>  The specific name of ''H. haiyanensis'' refers to Haiyan, the locality where it was discovered.<ref name=Yang2020description>{{harvnb|Yang|Kimmig|Lieberman|Peng|2020|p=3}}</ref>

==Description==
[[File:Herpetogaster collinsi reconstruction.png|thumb|left|Restoration]]
''Herpetogaster'' consisted of a sub-cylindrical segmented body that curved helically to the right like a ram's horn while narrowing posteriorly.  It had a short head segment that was broader than the body and featured a pair of branching tentacles emerging from either side of the anterior, with the mouth in between.  The head also featured pharyngeal openings, which were possibly lateral pores.  The body was supported by a flexible, extensible [[stolon]] emerging from somewhat lower than the middle of the trunk, with a terminal attachment disc.  The digestive system included a pharynx, a large stomach, and a narrow intestine that is un-looped and has triangular mesenterial insertions.  The intestine leads to a terminal anus at the tip of the last segment.<ref name=Yang2020description />

